   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g \\) be the function defined by \\( g(x) = \\int_{0}^{x} \\left(t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 8 t + 12\\right) \\, dt \\) for \\( -4 < x < 5 \\). Find the value of \\( x \\) at which \\( g \\) has a relative minimum."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -2
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, \\( g'(x) = x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 8 x + 12 \\) for \\( -4 < x < 5 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               -1
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               -6
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Set \\( g'(x) = 0 \\) and factor: \\( (x + 2)(x - 1)(x - 6) = 0 \\), so \\( x = -2, 1, 6 \\).",
         "mathjson": -2
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Only \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\) lie in \\( -4 < x < 5 \\); \\( x = 6 \\) is outside the interval on which \\( g \\) is defined, so it is not a critical point of \\( g \\).",
         "mathjson": -2
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "\\( g' \\) changes from negative to positive at \\( x = -2 \\) and from positive to negative at \\( x = 1 \\), so \\( g \\) has its relative minimum at \\( x = -2 \\).",
         "mathjson": -2
      }
   ],
